---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'install' as const;
---
<Docs lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Install the extension"
  description="From your browser store, from a release zip with no build chain, or from source.">
<div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>docs</b> / install</p>
          <h1>Install the extension</h1>
          <p class="lede measure">Three ways in, depending on what your machine lets you do. The first takes five minutes and needs nothing installed.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>From your browser's store — coming</h2>
          <p>Submission to the Chrome Web Store and to Firefox Add-ons has not happened yet, so there is nothing to link to and no listing to install from. Until then, the release zip below is the route.</p>
          <div class="callout warn">
            <span class="ct">why it matters, and not only for convenience</span>
            <h4>The store is the only channel that updates itself.</h4>
            <p>An extension loaded from a zip stays on the version you loaded until you repeat the operation by hand. For a team, that is the difference between everyone running the same version and nobody knowing who runs what — which is why this page says the channel is coming rather than quietly leaving it out.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>From a release zip — no Node, no Git</h2>
          <p>For a locked-down machine where you can't install a build chain. Every tagged release ships a ready-to-load zip, one for Chromium and one for Firefox.</p>
          <ul class="tick">
            <li><span class="mk">1</span><b>Download the bundle for your browser</b><span class="tx">From the <a href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">releases list</a> — the list, not <code>/releases/latest</code>, which skips prereleases. Take <code>…-chromium.zip</code> or <code>…-firefox.zip</code>.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">2</span><b>Unzip it somewhere other than Downloads</b><span class="tx">The browser re-reads that folder at every start, and deleting it disables the extension.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">3</span><b>Chrome, Edge, Brave…</b><span class="tx"><code>chrome://extensions</code> → <b>Developer mode</b> → <b>Load unpacked</b> → the unzipped folder, the one that directly contains <code>manifest.json</code>.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">4</span><b>Firefox — a different path</b><span class="tx"><code>about:debugging#/runtime/this-firefox</code> → <b>Load Temporary Add-on</b> → the <code>manifest.json</code> itself, not the folder. A temporary add-on is removed when Firefox closes.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <div class="callout blk">
            <span class="ct">the one thing to know</span>
            <h4>An extension loaded this way never updates itself.</h4>
            <p>This route is for trying it out, and for machines where the build chain can't be installed. It is not a distribution channel: you'll be on that version until you repeat the operation by hand. For a team, go through the stores.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>From source</h2>
          <p>If you want to read what you're running, or you're contributing.</p>
          <pre class="code">git clone https://github.com/reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit
cd conventional-comments-toolkit

npm ci
npm run build:extension     <span class="tk-cm">// → packages/extension/dist-ext</span></pre>
          <p>Then load <code>dist-ext</code> the way your browser wants it: step 3 above for Chromium, step 4 for Firefox. The Firefox variant is built alongside it, in its own subfolder.</p>
          <p class="small">Node 20 or later. There's a step-by-step Windows/PowerShell walkthrough in the repository, from installing Node to loading the extension.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Beyond github.com</h2>
          <p>Out of the box the extension only runs on github.com. Azure DevOps, a GitHub Enterprise Server instance, or an internal configuration host each need a permission you grant yourself.</p>
          <ul class="tick">
            <li><span class="mk">1</span><b>Open the options page</b><span class="tx">From the extension's icon in the toolbar.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">2</span><b>Add the domain, and say which platform it is</b><span class="tx">The extension can't guess whether <code>devops.acme.internal</code> is an Azure DevOps or a GitHub Enterprise instance — tagging it is what activates the right adapter.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">3</span><b>Confirm the permission your browser asks for</b><span class="tx">It's your browser asking, not the extension. You can revoke it there at any time.</span></li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Check that it works</h2>
          <p>Open any pull request and start a review comment. You should see the label row appear above the box, and a status dot below it. Type <code>?i</code> then <code>Tab</code>: it should expand to <code>issue:</code>.</p>
          <p>If nothing appears, the troubleshooting page covers the three usual reasons.</p>
        </section>
        <div class="pager"></div>
</Docs>
